"""LY05: original listening/viewing comprehension, supported interpretations."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
items=[]
def base(b,t,q):
 n=sum(x['bank']==b for x in items)+1
 text='Listen as an adult reads the text, or use Read aloud. You may listen again.\n\n'+t+'\n\n'+q
 return dict(id=f'AC9E3LY05-{b[0].upper()}-{n:03d}',subject='english',year_level='Year 3',curriculum_code='AC9E3LY05',bank=b,skill='Listening, viewing and comprehension',question=text,audio_prompt=text,visual={'type':'none'})
def mc(b,t,q,c,w,e):
 x=base(b,t,q);p=sum(a['bank']==b and a.get('grading_mode')!='adult-review' for a in items)%4;o=w.split(';');o.insert(p,c);x.update(answers=[{'text':s,'is_correct':j==p} for j,s in enumerate(o)],correct_index=p,explanation={'summary':e,'hint':'Listen again for the words that support the answer.'});items.append(x)
def adult(b,t,q,m,n,v=None):
 x=base(b,t,q);x.update(grading_mode='adult-review',answers=[],correct_index=None,model_answer=m,acceptance_note=n+' Confirm the learner listened to the supplied text and, when provided, viewed the image. Accept other evidence-supported wording.',response_instructions='Listen, then explain your answer aloud or in writing. Point to the details you used. An adult checks your understanding; listening again is allowed.',completion_label='I have given my answer after listening; an adult still needs to check it.',explanation={'summary':m,'hint':'Separate what the text says from what its clues suggest.'});
 if v:x['visual']=v
 items.append(x)
a='''The school bus will leave at nine. Bring a hat and a water bottle. Lunch will be provided.|What must the children bring?|A hat and a water bottle.|Their own lunch only.;A blanket and a torch.;A musical instrument.|The message names the hat and water bottle; lunch is provided.
Lena put seed trays on the sunny shelf and watered them. A week later, green shoots pushed through the soil.|Which event happened last?|Green shoots appeared.|Lena found the shelf.;Lena first watered the trays.;Lena put the trays on the shelf.|A week later places the shoots after the planting actions.
The puppy carried its empty bowl to the kitchen and waited beside the food cupboard.|What is the puppy most likely seeking?|Food.|A bath.;A new collar.;A walk in the rain.|The empty bowl and food cupboard support wanting food; the text does not state another need.
Our club meets on Tuesday. This week only, the meeting will be on Thursday because the room is being painted.|Which summary preserves the important change?|This week's Tuesday meeting moves to Thursday.|All future meetings are cancelled.;The club now meets every day.;The meeting stays on Tuesday this week.|The change is limited to this week and has a stated new day.
First fold the paper in half. Next draw half a heart beside the fold. Cut along the drawn curve, then open the paper.|What should happen immediately before opening the paper?|Cut along the curve.|Draw a whole star.;Colour the open paper.;Fold a second sheet.|The instruction sequence places cutting directly before opening.
Jai packed a spare pencil after his only pencil broke during yesterday's lesson.|Why does Jai probably pack the spare?|To have another pencil if one breaks.|To make his bag harder to carry.;To avoid doing any writing.;To give away every pencil.|Yesterday's problem supplies a reason for today's preparation.
For our model town, the library group made tiny books. The park group made trees. Both groups used leftover cardboard.|What do the two groups have in common?|They used leftover cardboard.|They both made trees.;They both made only books.;They used no materials.|The final sentence states their shared material, while their models differ.
Rain taps the window. / My warm mug steams. / Beyond the glass, / the garden dreams.|Which contrast is present in the poem?|Rain outside and warmth inside.|A loud argument and cheering.;Snow and a desert.;A crowded room and a busy road.|Rain beyond the window contrasts with the warm steaming mug.
The notice says, “Please walk beside the seedlings. Small feet can bend their stems.”|Why does the notice give the second sentence?|To explain the reason for walking beside the plants.|To name every plant.;To announce a running race.;To say the plants are fully grown.|The second sentence explains the possible harm that supports the request.
An old key lay beneath the mat. “That must be the missing one,” said Ivo. But the key was too large for the tiny lock.|Which clue makes Ivo's first idea doubtful?|The key is too large for the lock.|The key is old.;Ivo can speak.;The mat is on the ground.|Size directly conflicts with the idea that this key fits the lock.
The toy shop closes at five on weekdays and at four on Saturdays. It does not open on Sundays.|On which day is the shop closed all day?|Sunday.|Monday.;Friday.;Saturday.|The final sentence says it does not open on Sundays.
Ada described the path as “a silver ribbon winding between the trees”.|What does this description help the listener imagine?|A pale, narrow path curving through trees.|A real gift ribbon tied to every tree.;A path made of metal coins.;A straight road with no trees.|The comparison suggests shape and appearance, rather than literal ribbon material.'''
for r in a.splitlines():mc('practice',*r.split('|'))
p='''The picnic was planned for the oval. When dark clouds gathered, the class carried its baskets to the covered courtyard. Everyone still ate together.|Summarise the problem and the class's response in one or two sentences.|Clouds threatened the outdoor picnic, so the class moved to the covered courtyard and still ate together.|Require the weather concern, move and continued picnic; rain is not explicitly said to have fallen.
At the library, Sami searched the shelf labelled mysteries. He passed the joke books without stopping and borrowed another detective story.|What kind of book might Sami prefer? Give two clues and explain why this is an inference.|He may prefer mysteries: he searched that shelf and borrowed a detective story. His preference is suggested, not directly stated.|Require both clues and qualified language; passing joke books once does not prove dislike.
To borrow a costume, write your name beside its number. Return it clean by Friday. If it needs repair, tell the drama teacher.|What question would you ask if you wanted to know whether you could borrow two costumes? Explain why you need to ask.|May I borrow two costumes? The instructions explain recording and returning costumes but give no number limit.|Require a useful unanswered question, not an answer invented from the instructions.
The pond was still at dawn. By noon, ducks rippled the surface. At sunset, the water became smooth again.|Retell the changes in order using the time clues.|At dawn it was still; at noon ducks made ripples; at sunset it was smooth again.|Require all three stages in order; no explanation of duck behaviour is needed.
The first poster said, “Come to our play.” The revised poster added the date, the hall name and the starting time.|Evaluate one way the revision better helps families attend.|It gives when and where to go, such as the starting time and hall, so families can plan to attend.|Require a named added detail and its practical use, not only 'more information'.
A snail crossed the path carrying a leaf. At the puddle it stopped, placed the leaf on the water and climbed aboard.|Pause and predict a possible next event. Which clues support it?|The snail might float across the puddle on the leaf because it places the leaf on water and climbs aboard.|Require a plausible next event tied to actions; do not require that the prediction actually happens.
Part one: The cup trembled on the edge of the table. Part two: Before it fell, Jo moved it to the centre.|What prediction might you make after part one, and how does part two change it?|I might predict a fall from the edge, but Jo moves the cup to the centre, so that danger is reduced.|Require the initial clue and revision after new evidence.
The garden team needs helpers to carry light pots. Heavy bags will be moved by adults. Children should meet beside the shed.|A listener says children must carry the heavy bags. Correct that misunderstanding using the message.|Adults move the heavy bags; the children are asked to help with light pots.|Require the explicit role distinction.
The moon is a button / on night's dark coat. / A cloud slips past / like a silent boat.|Explain one comparison and what it helps you picture.|The moon is compared to a button on a dark coat, helping me picture a small bright shape against darkness.|Require one accurate comparison and its imagery effect; the poem is not a factual clothing claim.
Our recycling box is full. Please flatten clean cardboard before adding it. Flat pieces take up less space.|What is the request, and how does the final sentence support it?|Flatten clean cardboard; the space-saving explanation gives a reason to do it.|Require both request and stated reason.
Kira counted six chicks beside the hen. Another chick peeped from behind the bucket, where Kira could not see it.|How many chicks does the passage suggest are present? Explain the evidence and any uncertainty.|It supports seven: six chicks are seen, and the narrator tells us another chick is behind the bucket. Kira cannot see that chick yet.|Require six visible chicks plus the explicitly stated other chick; do not infer individuals from calls alone.
The river walk has three stops: the bridge, the old mill and the duck pond. At each stop, the guide will tell a short story.|What simple mental picture could help you remember the order?|I could imagine walking from a bridge to a mill and then to a pond, hearing a story at each.|Require correct sequence in the proposed visualisation, not drawing skill.
A note reads, “Dear visitors, please close the gate so our hens stay in the garden. Thank you.”|Who is addressed, and why does the note's polite request fit the situation?|Visitors are addressed. Please and thank you politely ask them to close the gate to keep hens in.|Require audience, request and fit of polite wording.
The old robot polished the same button every morning. When a child finally pressed it, music filled the workshop.|What earlier detail becomes more meaningful after the final event?|The robot's attention to the button matters because pressing it starts music; it was not just an unimportant decoration.|Require the button-to-music connection; the robot's motive is still unstated.
The leaflet has a heading, “Looking after your borrowed book”, followed by three short numbered tips.|How might this structure help a new borrower?|The heading tells the topic and numbered tips separate the actions so they are easy to follow or find.|Require specific structure and use; do not claim numbered tips always need a fixed order.
The class tried two paper bridges. One sagged under three counters. The folded bridge held six before bending.|State a comparison supported by these results, without claiming every folded bridge is stronger.|In this test, the folded bridge held more counters before bending than the other bridge.|Require bounded comparison from supplied numbers; no universal material claim.
“I didn't take it,” said the magpie. A shiny spoon poked from beneath its wing.|What makes the magpie's statement questionable? Can we know how the spoon got there?|The spoon under its wing conflicts with the denial, but the text does not tell how it got there.|Require visible/textual clue and uncertainty about the event; do not turn suspicion into proof.
The announcement says the swimming lesson is delayed, not cancelled. Families will receive the new time this afternoon.|Explain the difference between delayed and cancelled in this message.|Delayed means it is expected to happen later; the new time will be sent. Cancelled would mean it is not going ahead.|Require meaning from new-time context; do not invent the time.
Milo found a feather beside the nest. He wondered whether a bird had dropped it, but he had not seen a bird there.|Separate one observation from one possible explanation.|The feather beside the nest is observed. A bird dropping it is Milo's possible explanation, not something he saw.|Require fact/inference distinction without dismissing a plausible idea.
We need a quiet place for reading. The small room has soft chairs and a door that closes; the open hall is beside the drum practice.|Which place seems better for the stated purpose? Explain using two details.|The small room seems better because the door can close and the hall is near drumming; chairs also support reading comfortably.|Require purpose-based comparison with two supplied details.
A story begins with an empty birdcage. In the middle, a trail of seeds leads to the curtains. At the end, the bird chirps from the curtain rail.|Summarise the story using the beginning, clue and resolution.|A bird is missing from its cage. Seeds lead to the curtains, where the bird is found chirping on the rail.|Require three linked events, not every phrase repeated.
The sign says, “Use the side door today. The front steps are being repaired.” A visitor goes towards the front entrance.|What should the visitor do, and which sentence explains why?|Use the side door, because the front steps are being repaired.|Require both action and reason.
The musician played a slow tune while the puppet slept. When the puppet woke and danced, the tune became quick and bouncy.|How does the music's change help tell the story?|The slow tune matches sleep, while the quick bouncy tune matches the waking dance and makes the change clear.|Require both music-action pairings; no audio is supplied or claimed heard beyond reading this description.
A recipe note says, “Stir until the mixture is smooth.” Peta asks, “How will I know when to stop?”|Which words answer Peta's question, and what do they tell her to check?|Until the mixture is smooth tells her to check the mixture's texture rather than stop after an invented number of turns.|Require the stopping condition and avoid adding an unstated cooking time.
The narrator says, “I thought the box held shoes. Then it began to squeak.”|Explain how the second sentence changes the reader's expectation.|Squeaking makes the shoe assumption uncertain and suggests something unexpected, perhaps a toy or animal, but it is not revealed.|Require revision based on squeaking; accept plausible possibilities without treating them as facts.
The exhibition label reads, “These bowls were made by our class. Each pattern shows a favourite place.”|What can the label help a viewer understand that appearance alone may not show?|It tells who made the bowls and what the patterns represent: favourite places.|Require one or both stated meanings, no claim about an unseen particular pattern.
The field trip note has a packing list under one heading and the return time under another.|Why might separate headings be more useful than one long mixed paragraph?|A family can find what to pack separately from when the group returns, without searching through mixed information.|Require the two information needs and findability.
A tiny seed waited beneath the soil. / Rain knocked softly. / A green hand reached for morning.|What is likely happening in the poem? Link two lines to your interpretation.|A seed is sprouting: it begins under soil and a green shoot reaches upwards after rain.|Require seed and green-growth clues; green hand is figurative.
The game instructions explain how to score but do not say how many players are needed.|What should a listener ask before organising a game, and why?|How many people can play? The scoring information does not answer that planning question.|Require useful gap-based question, not invented player count.
A shop window says, “Every visitor receives a free bookmark today.” Inside, a sign adds, “One bookmark per visitor.”|How does the second sign clarify the first?|It explains the limit: each visitor receives one bookmark, not an unlimited number.|Require the scope of one per visitor, not a charge or membership rule.
The school report says 18 pupils walked to school on Monday and 12 on Tuesday. It gives no reasons.|What comparison can you make, and what question remains unanswered?|More pupils walked on Monday, by six. We cannot tell why the numbers changed.|Require correct comparison; arithmetic difference optional if comparison clear, and no invented weather cause.
The monster searched under its bed, shook out its slippers and sighed, “Where is my teddy?”|How does the final line help you reinterpret the earlier searching?|The monster is looking for a teddy, so the search may feel familiar or gentle rather than threatening.|Require teddy as the new clue; different supported tone responses accepted.
The first instruction says to draw a route. The next says, “Mark three landmarks along it.”|What does “it” refer to, and how does knowing that help follow the instruction?|It refers to the route, so landmarks should be marked along the drawn route.|Require correct reference and action.
An invitation says, “Bring a story to share, not a present.”|What might a guest misunderstand if they hear only “bring”, and how does the full sentence correct it?|They might assume a present is wanted, but the full instruction asks for a story and explicitly says not a present.|Require the complete instruction; no claim that every guest would misunderstand.
At the start, the kite maker worked alone. Later, neighbours brought sticks, paper and string. By evening, a giant kite was ready.|What main idea could a short summary emphasise? Support it.|Neighbours contribute different materials and help a project grow from one person's work into a finished giant kite.|Require shared contribution and outcome, not an unsupported claim that everyone flew it.
A visitor asks where the toilets are. The guide answers with the building's opening date and the architect's name.|Evaluate whether the answer meets the visitor's need. What should be added?|It does not answer where to go. The guide should give the toilets' location or directions.|Require relevance to the actual question, no invented location.'''
for r in p.splitlines():adult('practice',*r.split('|'))
# Forty-eight practice items reached; four viewing tasks replace the last four text tasks below.
# Preserve the removed situations for no other bank: distinct tests follow.
items=items[:-4]
t='''The notice says the art room is closed this morning and will reopen after lunch.|When may pupils use the art room again?|After lunch.|Before breakfast.;All morning.;Not for the rest of the week.|The reopening time is explicitly after lunch.
Maya put a bowl beneath the dripping tap and called an adult to help turn it off.|What problem is Maya responding to?|Water is dripping from the tap.|The bowl is missing.;The room has no water.;An adult has lost a key.|The dripping tap explains the bowl and request for help.
In the story, a lantern flashed twice from the tower. “That's our signal,” said the waiting sailor, raising the sail.|Which event causes the sailor to act?|The two lantern flashes.|The tower falls.;The sailor eats lunch.;The sea becomes dry.|The sailor identifies the flashes as the signal and then raises the sail.
The club's flyer says, “Try making a paper animal. Beginners welcome; materials supplied.”|Who is clearly welcome according to the flyer?|People who have not tried the activity before.|Only expert paper artists.;Only people bringing their own materials.;Nobody who needs paper.|Beginners welcome explicitly includes newcomers, and materials are supplied.'''
for r in t.splitlines():mc('test',*r.split('|'))
u='''First the children heard a rustle in the hedge. They expected a cat. Then a hedgehog's pointed nose appeared.|Explain how new evidence changes the first idea.|The rustle could have several causes, but the hedgehog's nose gives evidence that the animal is a hedgehog rather than the expected cat.|Require initial expectation and updating from the revealed animal.
The toy library asks borrowers to count the pieces before returning a puzzle. This helps the next borrower receive a complete set.|What is the request, and how does the explanation support it?|Count the puzzle pieces before returning it; this helps check the next borrower gets the full set.|Require action and purpose, no invented penalty.
A poem says, “The street stretched awake / as shutters lifted / and the first bus sighed.”|What change is the poem describing? Explain one non-literal expression.|The street is becoming active in the morning. Stretched awake gives the street a waking person's action, while shutters and a first bus support the change.|Require morning/activity clues and one explained expression.
A note says the concert starts at six, but performers should arrive at five-thirty for rehearsal.|A performer plans to arrive at six. Explain what they missed.|Six is the concert start; performers need to arrive at five-thirty for rehearsal.|Require the two roles/times without inventing duration.
The leaflet describes a walking route in numbered steps, each beginning with an action such as turn or cross.|How does this form help a person following the route?|The ordered actions guide the walker step by step and make the next movement easy to identify.|Require order and action-language usefulness.
A lost scarf hung on the library noticeboard. Nearby, a note asked its owner to speak to the librarian.|What can a viewer do if it is their scarf, and what does the text not tell us?|They can speak to the librarian. It does not tell who found the scarf or when it was lost.|Require stated next action and a genuinely unstated detail.
Two boxes are described. Box A has a clear label listing its contents. Box B has only a decorative pattern. You need to find spare pencils quickly.|Which feature would be more useful for your purpose, and why?|A contents label can tell whether pencils are inside; decoration alone does not identify them.|Require purpose-linked evaluation, not an unsupported claim that A definitely contains pencils.
The little dragon tried to blow a fierce flame. Instead, a shower of soap bubbles drifted out, and the knight began to laugh.|Explain how the unexpected event changes the tone.|The expected fierce flame becomes harmless bubbles, so the scene shifts towards humour rather than danger.|Require contrast and laughter; do not claim all dragons make bubbles.'''
for r in u.splitlines():adult('test',*r.split('|'))
# Four original viewing panels, two different demands each: two Practice, two Test.
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]
D=ROOT/'assets/assessment-visuals/year3/english/ly05';D.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
visuals=[('practice','p-map','<path d="M100 160L200 160L240 260H380L420 160H520" stroke="#4b7564" stroke-width="12" fill="none"/><rect x="55" y="80" width="110" height="70" fill="#ead6a6"/><text x="65" y="120">GATE</text><circle cx="310" cy="160" r="35" fill="#7bb9d5"/><text x="273" y="230">POND</text><rect x="455" y="80" width="130" height="70" fill="#d6a976"/><text x="464" y="120">SHED</text>','A path runs from the left gate below a central pond to the shed on the right.','The guide says, “Start at the gate. Pass the pond and meet me at the shed.”','Use the picture to describe the route in the correct order.','Start at the left-hand gate, follow the path around the middle pond and finish at the shed on the right.','Require gate→pond→shed, with no invented turn.','Someone stops at the pond. Explain how listening and viewing together show that the journey is not finished.','The spoken destination is the shed, and the picture shows it beyond the pond along the path.','Require the spoken destination and pictured position.'),('practice','p-seeds','<rect x="70" y="90" width="190" height="130" fill="#a9d38c"/><text x="90" y="125">SEEDS</text><circle cx="115" cy="160" r="8" fill="#65482e"/><circle cx="170" cy="165" r="8" fill="#65482e"/><circle cx="220" cy="180" r="8" fill="#65482e"/><path d="M290 160H340m-15-15 15 15-15 15" stroke="#333" fill="none" stroke-width="5"/><rect x="370" y="90" width="190" height="130" fill="#d6e8ef"/><text x="390" y="125">SHOOTS</text><path d="M425 200v-55m60 55v-55" stroke="#38723d" stroke-width="6"/><ellipse cx="410" cy="160" rx="18" ry="8" fill="#619e4c"/><ellipse cx="500" cy="160" rx="18" ry="8" fill="#619e4c"/>','A panel labelled Seeds points by an arrow to a panel labelled Shoots with green stems and leaves.','The class display says, “Our growing project: first seeds, then shoots.”','How do the arrow and the words work together?','The arrow runs from seeds to shoots, showing the same first-then order as the words.','Require direction and matching spoken sequence.','What useful question about the project cannot be answered by this display alone?','How many days did the shoots take to appear? The display shows stages but no time interval.','Accept a genuine missing detail, such as plant kind or watering amount, with explanation.'),('test','t-stalls','<rect x="50" y="75" width="230" height="155" fill="#f0d893"/><text x="75" y="115">BOOK SWAP</text><text x="85" y="170">10:00</text><rect x="350" y="75" width="230" height="155" fill="#c5e0e6"/><text x="370" y="115">PUPPET SHOW</text><text x="395" y="170">11:00</text>','Two panels show Book swap at 10:00 and Puppet show at 11:00.','The announcer says, “Visit the book swap before the puppet show. Check the display for starting times.”','Explain how the display supports the announced order.','The book swap starts at ten and the puppet show at eleven, so the displayed times put the swap first.','Require both times and correct order.','Can you tell when the book swap ends? Explain the limit of the display.','No. Ten is its starting time; no finishing time is shown.','Require distinction between start and end; do not assume one hour.'),('test','t-door','<rect x="70" y="50" width="190" height="200" fill="#cab699"/><text x="90" y="95">FRONT</text><path d="M110 130l95 80m0-80-95 80" stroke="#ad3f3f" stroke-width="10"/><rect x="390" y="50" width="190" height="200" fill="#b8d5a7"/><text x="425" y="95">SIDE</text><path d="M290 165h75m-20-20 20 20-20 20" fill="none" stroke="#284e39" stroke-width="8"/>','A door labelled Front has a large cross. An arrow points towards a door labelled Side.','The caretaker says, “The front entrance is closed today. Please use the side door.”','How do the cross and arrow help someone understand the message?','The cross marks the front as unavailable and the arrow directs attention to the side door, matching the spoken instruction.','Require both symbols and connection to spoken meaning.','What reason for the closure can you give from this information? Explain.','No reason is given. We know the front is closed today and should use the side, but cannot say why.','Require recognising missing cause, without inventing repairs or danger.')]
for b,name,art,alt,t,q,m,n,q2,m2,n2 in visuals:
 (D/(name+'.svg')).write_text('<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" id="scene" viewBox="0 0 640 300"><rect width="640" height="300" fill="#fffdf6"/><g font-family="sans-serif" font-size="22" fill="#25332c">'+art+'</g></svg>')
 v={'type':'svg','asset_path':f'/assets/assessment-visuals/year3/english/ly05/{name}.svg#scene','alt_text':alt}
